ry", "oldValue": "Lê Uy Mục, also called Lê Tuấn (黎濬), was the eighth emperor of the later Lê dynasty of Vietnam. He was the second son of Emperor Lê Hiến Tông and the elder half-brother of his direct predecessor, Emperor Lê Túc Tông.", "newValue": "Lê Uy Mục (born Lê Tuấn, 1488–1509) reigned as the eighth emperor of Đại Việt's Later Lê dynasty from 1505 to 1509, a period historically remembered as a stark departure from the dynasty's earlier prosperity. Ascending to the throne after the untimely death of his younger half-brother, Lê Túc Tông, Lê Uy Mục's brief rule became synonymous with extreme cruelty, moral decadence, and political instability. His reign marked a critical turning point, initiating the severe decline of the Later Lê dynasty that would culminate in its temporary overthrow by the Mạc dynasty two decades later.

Upon his ascension in 1505, Lê Uy Mục, driven by long-held resentments stemming from his mother's marginalization, unleashed a brutal purge within the imperial court. He ordered the execution of his own grandmother, Empress Dowager Trường Lạc, and numerous loyal officials and princes, consolidating power through terror. Historical accounts depict him as a tyrannical ruler who neglected state affairs, indulged excessively in lavish feasts, women, and alcohol, and delegated authority to corrupt eunuchs and his maternal relatives, the Nguyễn family. This systemic corruption and his personal depravity, which earned him the posthumous epithets 'Quỷ vương' (Devil King) and 'U vương' (King of Hell), alienated both the populace and the scholarly bureaucracy.

